<Layout title="The Audition" description="Read the prologue of The Diva and the Maestro: July 12, 1898, the morning Abbie Mitchell auditioned for Will Marion Cook on the roof of the Casino Theatre.">
  <div class="wrap page-title">
    <p class="eyebrow gilt">Prologue</p>
    <h1>The Audition</h1>
    <p class="lede">July 12, 1898. The roof of the Casino Theatre, Broadway and 39th Street.</p>
  </div>

  <article class="wrap reading">
    <div class="text">
<p>The morning of the audition, Abbie and Aunt Jo were both rather cross. It was a typical July morning in New York, extremely hot and muggy, and they'd had to wake up extra early to walk the 25 blocks from Aunt Jo's apartment in Greenwich Village to the Casino Theater on Broadway and 39th St.</p>
<p>"I'm already sweating," Abbie whined. "We should have bought the other dress."</p>
<p>"That dress showed too much skin, Abbie," snapped Aunt Jo. She tried to fan away the sweat beading on her dark brown face. "You are still a little girl, and I won't have a bunch of grown men looking at your naked bosom."</p>
<p>"If I am such a little girl, how come I have a bosom?" Abbie demanded. The dress they'd finally settled on was a full-skirted, ankle-length white challis with red polka dots, with lace around a high collar and elbow-length sleeves. It was very pretty, she thought, but more appropriate for a child going to a picnic than a young woman embarking on her first audition for the stage.</p>
<p>"I honestly don't know, but it's not right to show it off," admonished Aunt Jo. "Just be grateful you have a new dress for this crazy thing."</p>
<p>Aunt Jo had been dead set against the audition, relenting only because of Abbie's persistent begging coupled with her promise that auditioning would distract her from the recent sudden deaths of her beloved father and grandmother.</p>
<p>Abbie thought about dear Aunt Jo standing at the store counter counting out her pennies to pay for the dress and new shoes and felt bad about being so cranky.</p>
<p>"Thank you for everything, Aunt Jo. I'm sorry I am being a spoiled pill, I guess I'm just really scared," said Abbie.</p>
<p>Abbie's apology eased her aunt's bad mood. Jo gazed at her niece, who looked like a princess. Her thick black hair was gathered in a red ribbon at the nape of her neck and flowed down her back. Her skin was flawless and glowed light bronze in the sun. At fourteen, she really did have a woman's figure with a tiny waist and a full bust.</p>
<p>"You're scared to audition? Well, we can fix that right quick," said Aunt Jo. "Let's just turn around and go home."</p>
<p>Abbie threw back her head and laughed. Her Aunt was teasing her. Aunt Jo knew she'd cut off a limb before she'd miss this audition.</p>
<p>People turned around to see what the lovely girl was laughing at. Abbie paid no attention. She was used to people staring at her, admiring her, trying to figure out from what foreign country she might have come.</p>
<p>"Aunt Jo, you'll see. I am going to be a star. I'll be singing opera at the Met before you know it. Oh, it's a glorious morning and it's going to be a glorious day!" And it was a gorgeous New York morning, indeed, despite the heat. Abbie threw her arms to the sky and twirled around, bumping into a horse pulling a carriage full of women.</p>
<p>"Watch where you're going, girlie!" snapped the driver, yanking the reins to the left.</p>
<p>"Be careful Abbie, the streets are still a little damp," warned Aunt Jo, as she struggled to keep her own balance on the slick cobblestones.</p>
<p>How quickly can this girl's mood change, she thought. One minute she was sobbing herself to sleep in grief, the next she was prickly as a hedgehog, and the next, dancing in the streets, elated.</p>
<p>Jo sighed. She knew when she agreed to have Abbie visit her in New York for the summer it wouldn't be a cakewalk.</p>
<p>Sunlight glanced off the droplets of dew and rain on the roses, tulips, apples, peaches, oranges, and pears displayed in stands on every corner. Abbie stopped to admire the cornucopia of color and to inhale the aroma of fresh fruit and flowers mixed with the smell of rotting fruit and other garbage piled along the streets. The combination smelled like a pungent perfume.</p>
<p>"Only in New York could garbage smell delicious," Abbie declared. She was dying to buy a fresh peach and bite into it but didn't dare risk getting the juice on her dress.</p>
<p>"At least I got the boots I wanted," she thought, smiling to herself. They were gold, with black hooks and laces, pointed toes and a small, raised heel. As Aunt Jo had predicted, they were hard to walk in, but Abbie thought the overall effect almost made up for the childish dress.</p>
<p>They finally arrived at the theater an hour later, stopping outside to fan themselves before going in.</p>
<p>"Remember, we have to go up to the roof," said Abbie. Billy English, Aunt Jo's neighbor, had told them that's where performances were often held in the summer, so audiences and performers could find relief from the heat.</p>
<p>It was indeed cooler on the roof, and quite beautiful, with palm trees, ferns, boxes of flowers, rows of tables covered with white tablecloths, metal chairs and women cleaning to prepare for the night's performance. At the front was a stage, bare except for three men standing next to a large piano.</p>
<p>"Abbie, I'm going to sit down and rest my feet," said Aunt Jo. "You go on and good luck."</p>
<p>"Wait wh-wh-aat?" But Aunt Jo was already gone. She sank into a chair furthest from the stage.</p>
<p>"Probably trying to teach me a lesson for thinking I'm so grown," thought Abbie grimly.</p>
<p>She took a deep breath and slowly approached the men.</p>
<p>"Is one of you Mr. Cook?" Abbie asked, her voice quavering a little.</p>
<p>"Yes, I am," said the handsome one. He was tall and slender, with dark bushy hair and eyebrows, great burning eyes, a little black handlebar mustache, and a Black bowler perched on the side of his head.</p>
<p>"What do you want, girl?"</p>
<p>"Mr. Billy English sent me to sing for you. May I?"</p>
<p>"Hmmmmph," he said, looking her up and down, slowly. Abbie felt quite naked.</p>
<p>Will Marion was trying to figure out who this girl was and especially how old she was. He glanced over at Aunt Jo.</p>
<p>"Well, at least her mother is here," he thought. "I sure don't need problems with angry parents who don't know their daughters are auditioning."</p>
<p>He noted that although Abbie was small in stature, she had a woman's figure.</p>
<p>"Who did you say sent you?"</p>
<p>"Mr. Billy English. I believe he is a vaudeville performer. He lives in my aunt's apartment building, and he told us you were looking for girls for a chorus...?"</p>
<p>"Oh English," said Mr. Cook. "That fool. Well okay, let me introduce you to my esteemed colleagues." Cook gestured to the other two men to step forward. He presented them to Abbie with a flourish, as if to an audience.</p>
<p>"This is Paul Laurence Dunbar." He said in a voice mimicking an emcee at a vaudeville show. "He is a famous poet and novelist, who writes lyrics to my music. And this is Mr. Harry Burleigh, a great baritone singer and one of our foremost Negro composers. He is responsible for introducing the famous European composer Antonin Dvořák to Negro American music. You know Dvořák's music, of course."</p>
<p>Abbie nodded slightly, although she was pretty sure Cook knew she had never heard of Dvořák and was making fun of her.</p>
<p>"And I am Will Marion Cook, the most magnificent of them all!" he continued, in a still jocular manner. "I am creating a new genre of music that will outshine and outlive everyone else's. If you haven't heard of me yet, you will! Your ears will soon be ringing with the sound of my brilliance."</p>
<p>The other two men chuckled. Will Marion was fond of bragging about his talents, while also making fun of his reputation for being a conceited ass.</p>
<p>Good Lord this man is arrogant, thought Abbie, who didn't get the joke.</p>
<p>"How do you do," she said. She wasn't sure if it was appropriate to bow or curtsy, so she did a little of both and then felt awkward.</p>
<p>Dunbar was not as handsome as Will Marion, but had kind eyes, Abbie thought. Harry Burleigh was the shortest of the three, and a bit portly, with grey eyes and a brown mustache. Both men bowed to Abbie. She sensed that they didn't approve of the disrespectful and condescending way Cook spoke to her, but they didn't say anything.</p>
<p>"How old are you anyway?" demanded Cook. "What's your name? And where is your music? Can you sing any Negro songs?"</p>
<p>"No," Abbie replied curtly, a bit taken aback by the torrent of questions. "I'm Abbie Mitchell, and I'm 14. I sing in my church choir and at school, mostly religious and classical songs." She spoke clearly as she was taught, aware that Mr. Cook also spoke with a cultured accent in grammatically impeccable English.</p>
<p>"Really?? You only sing classics and church songs?" he asked derisively, glaring down at her. "That will get you far in this town." Cook laughed and pulled his hat further down over his eyes.</p>
<p>Cook's rudeness made Abbie feel braver.</p>
<p>"I don't care how tough New York is, I've just lost my precious Papa," she thought to herself. "There's nothing this city can do to hurt me more than I've already been hurt."</p>
<p>She straightened her shoulders and stared right into Will Marion's eyes.</p>
<p>"Why don't you hear me sing, sir?" she asked, in a respectful, but commanding tone. Will Marion looked surprised.</p>
<p>Mr. Burleigh spoke up in a reassuring baritone.</p>
<p>"Come here, little girl, sing a scale for us." Now this was a warm, sympathetic man, who reminded Abbie of her father, speaking in a tone she was accustomed to hearing from men, not like the nasty Mr. Cook.</p>
<p>Abbie sang several scales that took in the entire range of her voice. Then, with Mr. Burleigh on the piano, Abbie sang "Because I Love You, Dear" and her favorite, "Ave Maria," the song she had sung with her father at her grandmother's funeral. The men remained silent, listening.</p>
<p>After Abbie finished, Mr. Cook finally spoke, rather coldly. "You have a glorious voice little girl, plenty of fire, but you can't sing a damn thing!"</p>
<p>Abbie was stunned. She had thrilled people in the choirs at her school and church, and just recently, when she sat on Aunt Jo's fire escape and sang for her neighbors. Abbie believed she was as talented as the famous singer Sissieretta Jones (nicknamed "The Black Patti" after the famous opera singer Adelina Patti), the only Black woman who had ever performed at the White House. What kind of man was this?</p>
<p>"Come now Marion," cajoled Mr. Dunbar. Many of Will Marion's friends used his middle name as an affectionate nickname, particularly when trying to calm his notorious ire. "Be nice if you possibly can and play something for the little girl."</p>
<p>Apparently mollified, Mr. Cook sat down at the piano. He played and sang <em>Love in a Cottage</em> and <em>Darktown is Out Tonight</em>, two of his own compositions. Both had a syncopated beat, like Ragtime, but a more dignified sound, like classical music. Abbie had never heard any music like it before, and despite her growing antipathy for the man, she was impressed by his talent. He was also the most beautiful man she had ever seen in her short life.</p>
<InlinePlay item={darktown} lead="Hear what he played" />
<p>But the lyrics!? They were not the kind Abbie was accustomed to singing, and she wasn't sure she would want to. <em>Warm coons a prancing, swell coons a dancing, tough coons who want to fight, darktown is out tonight?</em> What would her conservative aunts, Alice and Jo, say to these lyrics, Abbie thought to herself. Or worse, the nuns at school?</p>
<p>"That is the kind of music you should sing. Negro music. And you are ashamed of it," Mr. Cook said in his gruff voice.</p>
<p>Abbie tried to defend herself. "Decent colored girls do not sing coon songs or ragtime," she said indignantly.</p>
<p>Mr. Cook glared at her. "These are not coon songs, nor ragtime," he said. "They are the most innovative music being performed today. They combine the best elements of traditional Negro music with classical music, into a sound that is all my own and will soon be the most popular music of all time. This is our sound, and it is magical. But perhaps you are too uppity to perform this music?"</p>
<p>Before Abbie could retort that she was certainly not uppity, Mr. Dunbar intervened.</p>
<p>"Marion, give the little girl a break," he said. "Where is your mother, little girl?"</p>
<p>"My aunt is here," Abbie said, and looked for Aunt Jo, hoping she would come to the rescue. But Jo only slumped down further in her chair.</p>
<p>Mr. Cook seemed to be deliberating. He instructed Abbie to turn around and sing a phrase he handed to her. Abbie did so, facing the rows of chairs where the audience would sit.</p>
<p>Suddenly, there was a voice from the back of the roof garden.</p>
<p>"She is good material, Will Marion. Give her thirty-five a week." The owner of the theater had climbed up to see what was going on. Abbie would later learn that George Lederer was a Jewish man and an important Broadway producer.</p>
<p>"I was told you were paying fifty." Abbie had no idea where she mustered the courage to say that.</p>
<p>"Ha!" said Mr. Cook. "We will give you thirty and see what you can do. Be here tomorrow at 8AM sharp. Now, what do you know about Clorindy?"</p>
<p>"Ummmmm, just that it's a new show...."</p>
<InlinePlay item={whoDat} lead="Hear Clorindy" />
<p>"It is, and historic as well. It is the very first, all-colored Broadway show. Produced, directed, and performed entirely by Black folk," he said proudly. "You will be able to say you were part of it, and that's worth money."</p>
<p>Abbie felt very fortunate indeed. Aside from the money, which would certainly help her family, this was an adventure that would take her mind off her grief. And it was an opportunity to get started in show business.</p>
<p>"It's a far cry from the Metropolitan Opera," she thought to herself. "But it does sound exciting and after all, I have to start somewhere." Even if she did have to work for this abrasive, conceited, ridiculously handsome man.</p>
<p>Will Marion pondered what had just occurred.</p>
<p>"I'm glad Lederer made the decision to hire this girl," he thought. "If it proves a disaster, he can't point the finger at me."</p>
<p>But Will had a larger concern. This man, who habitually and rather blatantly evaluated every woman he met as a potential sexual partner, was trying not to stare at Abbie. Normally he'd have no qualms about it, but this was a 14-year-old girl. Only a few years older than a child.</p>
<p>The thing was, Abbie didn't look 14. From the neck down, she looked at least 17 or 18. And while she wasn't his usual type, there was something about her that was exceptionally attractive. She had an energy, and a calm but steely determination, far beyond her years and even ability. She reminded Will of someone, but who?</p>
<p>And then it struck him. Abbie reminded him of himself, at about the same age. Abbie had a quieter type of confidence than cocksure young Will, but he had the impression that if crossed, if told she couldn't do something or have something she wanted, this girl would push relentlessly. She would go up, down, or sideways, over or under or around, no matter what the barriers. And she would probably get her way in the end.</p>
<p>"Just like me," he thought, with the satisfaction of encountering a kindred spirit. "Just like me."</p>
<p>Will had yet to learn that he and Abbie had other important things in common. Both had grown up swathed in security and love until their worlds were upended by the death of beloved caretakers. Abbie was still raw from the sudden deaths of her grandmother and father, just six months before the trip to New York. In Will's case it was his father, a lawyer and Negro rights activist, who died when Will was only ten years old.</p>
<p>As Will watched Abbie and her aunt walk towards the stairway, he thought back to young Will's hopes and dreams and grit and resolve, long before he met Paul Dunbar or Harry Burleigh or had any success on Broadway. He remembered a stubborn, reckless, proud, and bereft young boy on a train to Tennessee so many years ago.</p>
    </div>

